            "LLM_0": 93,
            "LLM_1": "GPT-5.4 Mini"
          }
        }
      ],
      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"The invention of the chainsaw is often attributed to two Scottish physicians, **John Aitken and James Jeffray**, in the late 18th century (around 1783–1785).\n\nHowever, their invention was not the power tool we know today. It was a hand-cranked device used for **symphysiotomy** (a surgical procedure to widen the pelvis during childbirth to assist in delivering a baby) and for excising diseased bone. It functioned like a chain-link saw that was operated by hand to make surgical cuts.\n\nThe evolution of the chainsaw into a tool for forestry happened much later:\n\n*   **Early 1900s:** Several inventors, including **Samuel J. Bens** (who received a patent for an \"endless chain saw\" in 1905) and **Andreas Stihl** (who patented the first electric chainsaw for felling trees in 1926), were responsible for developing the modern, portable version.\n*   **The Modern Chainsaw:** In 1927, **Emil Lerp**, a German engineer, developed the first gasoline-powered chainsaw. His company, **Dolmar**, became the first to mass-produce these machines.\n\nSo, while the medical origins go back to the 1780s, **Andreas Stihl and Emil Lerp** are generally credited with creating the portable gasoline-powered chainsaws used in the logging industry today.\"": [
        {
          "prompt": 94,
