      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \" The vacuum cleaner was not invented by a single person, but rather evolved through the contributions of several inventors over time. Here are the key figures:\n\n1. **Daniel Hess** (1860) – Often credited with the first carpet sweeper (not a true vacuum cleaner), which used rotating brushes and a bellows to create suction.\n\n2. **Hubert Cecil Booth** (1901) – Patented the first powered, motorized vacuum cleaner. His large, horse-drawn machine used an internal combustion engine to suck air through a cloth filter. He also coined the term \"vacuum cleaner.\"\n\n3. **James Murray Spangler** (1907) – A janitor who invented a portable, electric vacuum cleaner using a fan, a rotating brush, and a soapbox. He sold the patent to his cousin's husband, **William Henry Hoover**, who commercialized it and founded the Hoover Company.\n\n4. **James Dyson** (1978–1980s) – Revolutionized the design with his bagless, cyclonic vacuum cleaner that used centrifugal force to separate dust from air, preventing loss of suction.\n\nSo, while **Hubert Cecil Booth** is often credited with the first true electric vacuum cleaner, **Daniel Hess** made the earliest device, and **James Spangler** created the first practical home model that led to mass production.\"": [
