      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"The invention of the vacuum cleaner wasn't the work of a single person, but rather a series of inventions that evolved over time. Here is the breakdown of the most significant contributors:\n\n### 1. The Early Manual Designs (The \"Carpet Sweepers\")\nBefore motorized vacuums, inventors created manual suction devices. \n*   **Daniel Hess (1860):** An American from Iowa, Hess patented the \"carpet sweeper,\" which featured a rotating brush and a bellows mechanism to create suction. It is considered the first true vacuum cleaner design, though it’s unclear if it was ever mass-produced.\n*   **Melville Bissell (1876):** While not a vacuum, his invention of the manual carpet sweeper was a huge success and paved the way for floor-cleaning interest.\n\n### 2. The First Motorized Vacuums\nThe transition to powered machines happened around the turn of the 20th century:\n*   **Hubert Cecil Booth (1901):** A British engineer, Booth is often credited with inventing the **first powered vacuum cleaner.** After watching a demonstration of a machine that blew dust off chairs, he theorized that *sucking* air into a filter would be more effective. He built a massive, horse-drawn machine called \"Puffing Billy\" that ran on a petrol engine. It was so large it had to stay outside, with long hoses run through the windows of buildings. \n*   **John S. Thurman (1899):** Around the same time, Thurman designed a gasoline-powered \"pneumatic carpet renovator.\" However, it blew dust away rather than sucking it up, functioning more like an air compressor.\n\n### 3. The Pioneer of Portability\n*   **James Murray Spangler (1907):** The modern \"upright\" vacuum as we know it was largely the brainchild of Spangler, a janitor in Canton, Ohio. He suffered from asthma and was tired of the dust kicked up by his sweeper. He combined an electric fan, a box, and one of his wife's pillowcases as a dust filter. \n\n### 4. Commercialization\n*   **William Hoover (1908):** Spangler took his invention to his cousin, who was married to William Hoover. Hoover saw the potential, bought the patent from Spangler, and founded the **Hoover Company**. It was through the Hoover brand that the vacuum cleaner became a household appliance.\n\n**Summary:** While **Hubert Cecil Booth** created the first suction-based motorized machine, **James Murray Spangler** invented the portable electric vacuum that eventually launched the **Hoover** empire.\"": [
