      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"# Back-Propagation Algorithm\n\nBack-propagation was invented by **David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton, and Ronald Williams** in **1986**. \n\nTheir seminal paper, \"Learning Representations by Back-propagating Errors,\" published in *Nature*, was instrumental in reviving interest in neural networks and deep learning. The algorithm provided an efficient method for training multi-layer neural networks by computing gradients and updating weights through the chain rule.\n\nHowever, it's worth noting that the mathematical foundations had been developed earlier by others, including Paul Werbos (1974), but Rumelhart, Hinton, and Williams' 1986 publication is widely credited as the breakthrough that made back-propagation practical and widely adopted.\"": [
