      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"The back-propagation algorithm, in the form used today to train neural networks, does not have a single inventor; rather, it was developed and refined by several researchers over several decades.\n\nThe history of its development is generally viewed in three stages:\n\n**1. The Mathematical Foundation (1960s–1970s)**\nThe fundamental mathematical concepts were developed in the context of control theory by **Henry J. Kelley (1960)** and **Arthur E. Bryson (1961)**. Later, in 1970, **Seppo Linnain](\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seppo_Linnainmaa\")** published the first general method for automatic differentiation of discrete connected networks of nested differentiable functions (which is essentially what back-propagation is).\n\n**2. The Application to Neural Networks (1974)**\n**Paul Werbos** is widely credited with being the first to propose the application of this method to train artificial neural networks in his 1974 Harvard PhD thesis. He explicitly noted the potential for training networks to learn internal representations, though his work did not receive widespread attention at the time.\n\n**3. Popularization (1986)**\nThe algorithm became famous in the scientific community due to the seminal 1986 paper, *\"Learning representations by back-propagating errors,\"* written by **David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton, and Ronald Williams**. While they did not \"invent\" the math, they demonstrated that back-propagation could allow hidden layers in neural networks to develop useful internal representations of data, which effectively reignited interest in neural networks and launched the modern era of deep learning.\n\n**In summary:**\n*   **Seppo Linnainmaa** is credited with the first general implementation of the technique (reverse-mode automatic differentiation).\n*   **Paul Werbos** was the first to apply it to neural networks.\n*   **Rumelhart, Hinton, and Williams** are responsible for popularizing it as a practical tool for AI.\"": [
