      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"The development of the back-propagation algorithm was not the work of a single person, but rather a process that spanned several decades and involved multiple researchers.\n\nHere is the breakdown of its history:\n\n### 1. Early Mathematical Foundations (1960s – 1970s)\nThe mathematical foundations were laid by **Henry J. Kelley** (1960), **Arthur E. Bryson** (1961), and **Stuart Dreyfus** (1962), who developed the basis for continuous back-propagation in the context of control theory. \n\nIn 1970, **Seppo Linnainmaa** published the first general method for the automatic differentiation of discrete connected networks of nested differentiable functions. This is widely considered the first implementation of the reverse mode of automatic differentiation, which is the core mechanism of back-propagation.\n\n### 2. Application to Neural Networks (1974)\nThe first person to suggest applying this approach to learning in artificial neural networks was **Paul Werbos**. In his 1974 Harvard PhD thesis, he described the process of using back-propagation to update weights in neural networks, though his work did not receive widespread attention at the time.\n\n### 3. Popularization (1986)\nThe back-propagation algorithm became famous and widely adopted after a 1986 paper titled *\"Learning representations by back-propagating errors\"* was published in the journal *Nature*. \n\nThe authors of this landmark paper were **David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton, and Ronald Williams**. While they did not invent the algorithm from scratch, their work demonstrated that back-propagation could allow hidden layers of neurons to learn internal representations, effectively solving the \"XOR problem\" that had caused a stagnation in neural network research. This paper is largely responsible for making back-propagation the standard training method for multi-layer perceptrons used today.\n\n***\n\n**Summary:** \n*   **Seppo Linnainmaa** is credited with the first general form of the algorithm (automatic differentiation).\n*   **Paul Werbos** was the first to propose it for neural network training.\n*   **Rumelhart, Hinton, and Williams** are credited with its popularization and demonstrating its practical power in deep learning.\"": [
