      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"There is no single person who invented \"the\" coffee maker, as the process evolved through several inventions over centuries as people moved away from simply boiling coffee grounds in a pot.\n\nHere are the key milestones and the inventors associated with them:\n\n### 1. The First Drip Method (1800)\nThe first documented coffee maker was invented in **1800** by **Jean-Baptiste de Belloy**, the Archbishop of Paris. He created the \"drip\" method (the *drip pot* or *biggin*), which used a perforated metal plate to filter grounds. This is considered the direct ancestor of modern drip coffee makers.\n\n### 2. The First Espresso Machine (1884)\nThe quest for a faster way to brew led to the first steam-pressure machine. In **1884**, **Angelo Moriondo** of Turin, Italy, received a patent for an \"instantaneous\" steam-driven coffee beverage maker. While his machine was large and meant for commercial use, it laid the groundwork for modern espresso.\n\n### 3. The Perfection of Espresso (1901)\nIn **1901**, **Luigi Bezzera** improved upon Moriondo’s design by creating the first single-shot espresso machine. He is often credited with the invention of the modern espresso machine because he introduced the \"portafilter\" and the mechanism that forces water through coffee under pressure.\n\n### 4. The Moka Pot (1933)\nIf you are thinking of the classic stovetop \"Moka Pot\" found in many kitchens today, that was patented in **1933** by **Alfonso Bialetti**. It utilized simple steam pressure and became a design icon of Italy.\n\n### 5. The Automatic Drip Machine (1954/1972)\n*   **1954:** The first successful automatic drip coffee maker, the **Wigomat**, was invented in Germany by **Gottlob Widmann**. \n*   **1972:** The **Mr. Coffee** machine, invented by **Vincent Marotta**, popularized the automatic drip machine in American homes. It was the first machine to heat water internally and drip it through a filter basket automatically, making it the standard for the modern household.\n\n**In summary:** If you are asking who invented the concept of filtering coffee, it is **Jean-Baptiste de Belloy**. If you are asking who invented the modern machine many people use every morning, it is a combination of **Gottlob Widmann** (the mechanism) and **Vincent Marotta** (the mass-marketed home device).\"": [
