      "Return only a comma-separated list of named entities that appear in the following text: \"There is no single person who invented the \"coffee maker,\" as the process of brewing coffee evolved through several different inventions over centuries.\n\nHere are the key milestones and the individuals credited with the major breakthroughs:\n\n### 1. The First Patent (The Percolation Method)\nThe first person credited with inventing a device specifically for brewing coffee was **Jean-Baptiste de Belloy**, the Archbishop of Paris, in **1800**. He invented the **drip coffee pot** (known as the *cafetière à la Belloy*). It used a metal strainer containing ground coffee through which hot water was poured.\n\n### 2. The Steam/Pressure Invention (The First Espresso Machine)\nIn **1822**, French inventor **Louis Bernard Rabaut** created the first machine that brewed coffee using steam pressure. However, it was early and lacked the precision of modern machines. \n\n### 3. The Percolator\nIn **1819**, a tinsmith named **Joseph-Henry-Marie Laurens** invented the first percolator, though it was later improved by **James Nason** in 1865, who patented the now-familiar \"percolator\" design in the United States.\n\n### 4. The Modern Espresso Machine\nThe foundation for the modern espresso machine was laid by **Angelo Moriondo** of Turin, Italy. In **1884**, he was granted a patent for a \"steam-operated machinery for the economic and instantaneous confection of coffee beverage.\" \n\nLater, in 1901, **Luigi Bezzera** improved upon Moriondo’s design by adding a portafilter and individual group heads, making the process much faster and closer to the modern espresso shot. In 1905, **Desiderio Pavoni** bought Bezzera’s patent and began mass-producing the machine (the *Ideale*).\n\n### 5. The Automatic Drip Coffee Maker (Home Use)\nThe electric drip coffee maker, which became the standard in American homes, was invented by **Gottlob Widmann** in 1954 (the *Wigomat*). Before this, coffee makers were largely stove-top or required manual pouring.\n\n***\n\n**Summary:** \n* If you mean the **concept of drip coffee**: **Jean-Baptiste de Belloy (1800)**.\n* If you mean the **espresso machine**: **Angelo Moriondo (1884)**, with significant improvements by **Luigi Bezzera (1901)**.\n* If you mean the **modern home electric drip machine**: **Gottlob Widmann (1954)**.\"": [
